==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|aland}}, {{m|enm|alond}}, {{m|enm|alonde}}, {{m|enm|o lande}}, from {{der|en|ang|on lande|t=on land}}, equivalent to {{af|en|a-|land}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈland/|a=UK}}
* {{rhymes|en|ænd|s=2}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} On dry [[land]], as opposed to in the water. {{defdate|13th|19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Pericles|V|text=I maruell how the Fishes liue in the Sea {{...}} Why, as Men doe '''a-land'''.}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare|poetic}} To the [[land]]; [[ashore]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1541|title=The Chronicle of Calais|location=London|year_published=1846
|passage=Henry the Eighth {{...}} departed out of England from Sowthampton, with a great navy of shipps to set that company '''aland''' in Spayne, for to helpe the kynge of Spayne agaynste the Frenche kynge {{...}}}}

